import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Navbar from "../components/Navbar.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Trust & Security — Datanika"
  description="Datanika's security architecture, compliance posture, data protection practices, and infrastructure overview. Hosted in the EU, open-source and auditable, every sub-processor named."
>
  <Navbar />
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-6 py-20">
    <h1 class="mb-2 text-4xl font-bold gradient-text">Trust & Security</h1>
    <p class="mb-10 text-lg text-slate-400">
      How we protect your data, your credentials, and your pipelines.
    </p>

    <div class="space-y-12 text-slate-300 leading-relaxed [&_h2]:text-2xl [&_h2]:font-semibold [&_h2]:text-white [&_h2]:mb-4 [&_h3]:text-lg [&_h3]:font-medium [&_h3]:text-white [&_h3]:mb-2 [&_ul]:list-disc [&_ul]:pl-6 [&_ul]:space-y-2">

      <!-- Security Architecture -->
      <section>
        <h2>Security Architecture</h2>
        <p class="mb-4">
          Security is built into every layer of Datanika — from how we store credentials
          to how we isolate tenants. Our codebase is open-source, so every security claim
          below is verifiable in code.
        </p>

        <div class="grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2">
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/50 p-5">
            <h3>Multi-tenant isolation</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-400">
              All tables use <code class="text-violet-400">org_id</code> column filtering
              via <code class="text-violet-400">TenantMixin</code>. Every query is scoped
              to the authenticated organization. A dedicated
              <strong class="text-slate-300">cross-tenant isolation suite</strong> verifies
              that Org A cannot read, update, or delete Org B's data across connections,
              uploads, runs, and notifications, with additional
              <code class="text-violet-400">wrong_org</code> coverage in every service
              test module.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/50 p-5">
            <h3>Credential encryption</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-400">
              Connection credentials (API keys, database passwords, service account JSON)
              are encrypted at rest using <strong class="text-slate-300">Fernet</strong>
              symmetric encryption. Credentials are decrypted only at pipeline runtime,
              never logged, and never exposed in the UI after initial save.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/50 p-5">
            <h3>Authentication</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-400">
              Passwords are hashed with <strong class="text-slate-300">bcrypt</strong>.
              Sessions use <strong class="text-slate-300">JWT</strong> tokens. API access
              uses scoped API keys. SSO is available via <strong class="text-slate-300">SAML 2.0</strong>
              and <strong class="text-slate-300">OIDC</strong> on the Enterprise plan.
              OAuth login with Google and GitHub is available on all plans.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/50 p-5">
            <h3>Authorization</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-400">
              Role-based access control with four roles: <strong class="text-slate-300">Owner</strong>,
              <strong class="text-slate-300">Admin</strong>,
              <strong class="text-slate-300">Editor</strong>, and
              <strong class="text-slate-300">Viewer</strong>. Permissions are enforced at
              both the UI state layer and the service layer — no client-side-only guards.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/50 p-5">
            <h3>Audit logging</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-400">
              All mutations (create, update, delete) are logged with user ID, organization,
              action type, and timestamp. Audit logs are queryable per-org and retained
              for the lifetime of the account.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/50 p-5">
            <h3>Security testing</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-400">
              <strong class="text-slate-300">112 security test functions</strong> across
              11 dedicated test files covering authentication, injection, path traversal,
              tenant isolation, rate limiting, token security, file-upload security, CSRF,
              input validation, and API-key security. Every push to <code class="text-violet-400">dev</code>
              runs the full suite. Count taken 2026-08-30 — the suite grows, so
              <a href="https://github.com/datanika-io/datanika-core/tree/master/tests/test_security" class="text-violet-400 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-violet-300">count them yourself</a>
              rather than take ours.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Compliance -->
      <section>
        <h2>Compliance</h2>

        <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/50 p-6 mb-6">
          <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
            <div class="flex-shrink-0 mt-1">
              <svg class="h-6 w-6 text-slate-400" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z" />
              </svg>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-1">SOC 2 — no attestation, and no date</h3>
              <p class="text-sm text-slate-400">
                We hold no SOC 2 attestation, Type I or Type II, and we are not publishing
                a target date for one. Until 2026-08-30 this page carried a SOC 2 Type I
                commitment with a stated completion target of
                <strong class="text-slate-300">Q3 2026</strong>. No auditor had been engaged
                behind that date. We withdrew it rather than let it stand, and we are
                recording the withdrawal here rather than quietly deleting the sentence —
                anyone who read the earlier page should be able to see what changed.
                If an attestation is a hard requirement for your procurement process today,
                we are not the right vendor for you yet, and we would rather say so now than
                at the end of a security review.
              </p>
              <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 mt-2">
                What we can point at instead is checkable without taking our word for it.
                The security test suite described above runs on every push. The core is
                open source, so every claim on this page can be read in the code that
                implements it — including the ones that are inconvenient for us. Every
                sub-processor is named below, with what it receives and where it sits,
                and the change log records when that list was wrong. And when we fix a
                security issue we say so in the release notes rather than shipping it
                quietly; our disclosure policy is in
                <a href="https://github.com/datanika-io/datanika-core/blob/master/SECURITY.md" class="text-violet-400 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-violet-300">SECURITY.md</a>.
              </p>
              <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 mt-2">
                Evaluating Datanika and need to go further than this page — a security
                questionnaire, a DPA, or a walkthrough of a specific control? Email
                <a href="mailto:security@datanika.io" class="text-violet-400 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-violet-300">security@datanika.io</a>.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white">GDPR:</strong> the application, its database and its
            off-site backups are all in Greece, in the EU. Data Processing Agreement (DPA)
            available on request — email
            <a href="mailto:info@datanika.io" class="text-violet-400 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-violet-300">info@datanika.io</a>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white">Data residency:</strong> your account data and your
            pipeline data are stored in Greece (EU) and backed up to a second Greek provider.
            Several sub-processors operate outside the EU and receive only what their function
            needs — <strong class="text-slate-300">Resend</strong> (US) receives the recipient
            address on transactional email, <strong class="text-slate-300">Cloudflare</strong>
            (US, global points of presence) terminates TLS and so sees IP addresses and request
            metadata, <strong class="text-slate-300">Paddle</strong> (UK) holds billing
            details, and <strong class="text-slate-300">Google</strong> (US) receives mail you
            send to our published contact addresses.
            The Resend, Cloudflare and Paddle transfers rely on the Standard Contractual Clauses
            in each provider's DPA, on their EU–U.S. Data Privacy Framework certifications, and
            on the UK adequacy decision; the contact mailbox is an ordinary Google mailbox and is
            not covered by an agreement of that kind.
            <strong class="text-slate-300">GitHub</strong> (US) and
            <strong class="text-slate-300">Telegram</strong> (outside the EEA) are also
            established outside the EU but receive no customer records — our source code and
            our infrastructure alerts respectively. Data also leaves the EU when you configure it to — pointing a
            pipeline at a US-region BigQuery destination, for example. Full breakdown in
            <a href="/privacy" class="text-violet-400 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-violet-300">the privacy policy</a>,
            section 9.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white">Data retention and deletion:</strong> pipeline run
            history, run logs and configuration metadata are retained for as long as the
            organization exists — we do not currently run a time-based purge of run logs.
            Organizations and connections use a soft-delete model: records are marked deleted
            and removed from all application code paths, but the underlying Fernet-encrypted
            data remains in the database until a hard delete is run.
            <strong class="text-white">Personal data is not soft-deleted.</strong> Deleting your
            account from Settings is self-service and erases it outright, synchronously — the rule
            the code follows is that a row identifying a <em>person</em> is hard-deleted while a row
            identifying a <em>record</em> is soft-deleted. Deleting an <em>organization</em> is not
            self-service: hard deletion of an organization and all
            associated data is done by us on request — email
            <a href="mailto:security@datanika.io" class="text-violet-400 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-violet-300">security@datanika.io</a>,
            and personal data is removed within 30 days, including from the off-site backups,
            whose retention window is exactly 30 days.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Infrastructure -->
      <section>
        <h2>Infrastructure</h2>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800">
                <th class="text-left py-3 pr-4 text-slate-400 font-medium">Layer</th>
                <th class="text-left py-3 pr-4 text-slate-400 font-medium">Provider</th>
                <th class="text-left py-3 text-slate-400 font-medium">Details</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-slate-300">
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800/50">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-white">Application hosting</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">Pointer (pointer.gr)</td>
                <td class="py-3">Virtual private server in Greece (EU). Ubuntu 22.04 LTS, 4 vCPU / 8 GB.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800/50">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-white">Off-site backups</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">Aweb</td>
                <td class="py-3">Second provider, also in Greece (EU). Nightly database dump, 30-day retention.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800/50">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-white">CDN & DDoS protection</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">Cloudflare</td>
                <td class="py-3">All traffic proxied. WAF rules, rate limiting, bot management.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800/50">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-white">TLS</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">Cloudflare + Apache</td>
                <td class="py-3">
                  TLS 1.2+ enforced. Cloudflare Origin Certificate for the backend. HSTS enabled.
                  Apache 2.4 is the reverse proxy on the application server; this marketing site is
                  served by nginx. This row is about traffic <em>to</em> Datanika. Traffic from
                  Datanika out to a database you host is a different question, answered per
                  connector in
                  <a href="#encryption-in-transit" class="text-violet-400 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-violet-300">Encryption in transit</a>.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800/50">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-white">Database</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">PostgreSQL 16</td>
                <td class="py-3">
                  Runs on the application host itself and is not published to the network —
                  the measure stated in Annex II of our
                  <a href="/dpa" class="text-violet-400 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-violet-300">DPA</a> as
                  <em>"internal services bind to loopback only"</em>. Nightly automated
                  backups, kept 7 days locally and 30 days off-site.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800/50">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-white">Transactional email</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">Resend</td>
                <td class="py-3">Password resets and team invitations, over SMTP with TLS. United States.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800/50">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-white">Monitoring</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">Prometheus + Grafana</td>
                <td class="py-3">Self-hosted on the application server. Container metrics, resource utilization, alerting.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-white">Analytics</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">Plausible CE + Cloudflare Web Analytics</td>
                <td class="py-3">
                  Both are cookie-free: no cookies, no browser storage, no cross-site identifier,
                  no advertising or behavioural profile. Plausible CE is self-hosted on our own
                  Greek server. Cloudflare Web Analytics runs on this marketing site only, for
                  page-load timings. Cloudflare already terminates TLS for every request you make
                  to us, so this is an additional function performed by a sub-processor we already
                  use — not a new company receiving your data.
                </td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Encryption in transit -->
      <section id="encryption-in-transit">
        <h2>Encryption in transit</h2>

        <p>
          Connections to Datanika always use TLS. Connections from Datanika out to a database you
          host are encrypted only where the connector and your server support it, and we do not yet
          verify your server's certificate.
        </p>

        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-400">
          Those are two different questions and we used to answer only the first. Until 2026-09-20
          this page's sibling documents said that all data in transit was encrypted, which was not
          true of the second. The table below is the honest answer, per connector, and it is the one
          to quote in a security review. We tighten these rows as fixes ship rather than restating
          them — SQL Server and Synapse moved from the first column to the third this way.
        </p>

        <div class="mt-5 overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800">
                <th class="text-left py-3 pr-4 text-slate-400 font-medium">Connector</th>
                <th class="text-left py-3 pr-4 text-slate-400 font-medium">Connection from Datanika</th>
                <th class="text-left py-3 pr-4 text-slate-400 font-medium">Your server's certificate</th>
                <th class="text-left py-3 text-slate-400 font-medium">How we know</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-slate-300">
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800/50">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-white">PostgreSQL</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">
                  Encrypted when your server offers TLS. Against a server that does not, the
                  connection succeeds in plaintext and reports success. The connection form has no
                  setting that requires encryption.
                </td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">Not verified</td>
                <td class="py-3">Measured</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800/50">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-white">Amazon Redshift</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">
                  Reached with the same PostgreSQL driver and the same defaults as the row above.
                </td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">Not verified</td>
                <td class="py-3">
                  Reasoned. Measured against PostgreSQL, not against a Redshift cluster.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800/50">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-white">MySQL</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">
                  Not encrypted. A server that <em>requires</em> TLS cannot be connected to at all,
                  and the connection form has no setting that can ask for it.
                </td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">—</td>
                <td class="py-3">Measured</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800/50">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-white">Oracle</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">
                  Not encrypted, and the connection form has no setting that can ask for it.
                </td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">—</td>
                <td class="py-3">Measured</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800/50">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-white">SQL Server</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">
                  Encrypted. The driver configuration we ship requires it, in both directions.
                </td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">Not verified</td>
                <td class="py-3">Measured</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800/50">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-white">Azure Synapse</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">
                  Encrypted, through the same driver as SQL Server. Synapse requires encryption at
                  its end as well.
                </td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">Not verified</td>
                <td class="py-3">
                  Reasoned. Measured against SQL Server, not against a Synapse workspace.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800/50">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-white">ClickHouse</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">
                  Plaintext unless you tick <strong class="text-slate-300">Use HTTPS (TLS)</strong>
                  on the connection. This is the one connector where encryption is your choice.
                </td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">
                  Verified against public certificate authorities when TLS is on. There is no field
                  for a private or self-signed authority, so a server using one cannot be connected
                  to over TLS.
                </td>
                <td class="py-3">Measured</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800/50">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-white">MongoDB</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">
                  Not encrypted. The connection form carries no TLS control, so a deployment that
                  requires TLS — Atlas, Cosmos DB's Mongo API, a self-hosted
                  <code class="text-violet-400">requireTLS</code> — cannot be connected to at all.
                </td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">—</td>
                <td class="py-3">Measured</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800/50">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-white">BigQuery, Snowflake, Databricks</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">
                  HTTPS, through each vendor's own client library. Datanika passes no transport
                  options to any of the three.
                </td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">Verified, by those libraries' own defaults</td>
                <td class="py-3">
                  <strong class="text-slate-300">Reasoned</strong>, from the client libraries we
                  install. We have not measured these three against the live services.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-white">DuckDB, SQLite</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">No network connection — a local file.</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">—</td>
                <td class="py-3">—</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-slate-400">
          <strong class="text-slate-300">What "not verified" costs you.</strong> Encryption without
          certificate verification keeps the data from being read by someone watching the network.
          It does not prove that the machine answering is yours, so it does not defend against
          someone on the network path who can pose as your server. Verification is not added for
          the self-hosted databases above because it would refuse every self-signed certificate,
          which is the default on a great many on-premises servers — so it is a change that has to
          arrive with a way for you to supply your own certificate authority, not before it.
        </p>

        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-400">
          <strong class="text-slate-300">Scope.</strong> The table covers the connections a pipeline
          opens to extract and to load. Transformations run through dbt's own adapters and open
          their own connections; those were not measured separately. Kafka is not a database and is
          not in the table: its transport is set per connection and is documented on
          <a href="/docs/connectors/kafka" class="text-violet-400 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-violet-300">the Kafka page</a>.
          Nothing here was measured against a hosted service — the measurements were taken against
          servers we stood up for the purpose, and the rows that rely on a vendor's behaviour rather
          than on our own reading say so.
        </p>

        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-400">
          <strong class="text-slate-300">Datanika's own database.</strong> Everything above is about
          connections to databases <em>you</em> host. Our own PostgreSQL is a separate question, and
          we would rather answer it here than in a security review: connections between the Datanika
          application and its own database are
          <strong class="text-slate-300">not TLS-encrypted</strong>. That database is not reachable
          from outside the server, and this traffic never leaves the host. The application
          authenticates to it with a hashed password
          (<code class="text-violet-400">scram-sha-256</code>), so no credential is sent in the clear
          even though the session is not. We will not describe this connection as encrypted until it
          is.
        </p>
      </section>

